{{Short description|Type of bead used to secure an inro in one's belt}}
{{Italic title}}
[[File:Ivory netsuke of the Hare with Amber Eyes 2.JPG|thumb|[[The Hare with Amber Eyes]] {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}, by Masatoshi, Osaka, {{circa|1880}}, signed. Ivory, amber buffalo horn]]
A {{nihongo|||'''netsuke'''|extra={{IPAc-ja|ne|tsu|*|ke}}}} is a miniature sculpture, originating in 17th century [[Japan]]. Initially a simply-carved button fastener on the cords of an {{transliteration|ja|[[inrō]]}} box, {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} later developed into ornately sculpted objects of craftsmanship.<ref name ="saip86">Yuji Yamashita (2014), ''Meiji no saimitsu kogei''. pp. 86-87. 
[[Heibonsha]] {{ISBN|978-4582922172}}</ref>

== History ==
[[File:印籠-Inro with Design of Eulalia Grass and Deer.jpg|thumb|An ivory {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} with an eagle design and an {{transliteration|ja|inrō}} with a deer design. The combination of {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} and {{transliteration|ja|inrō}} creates a story of an eagle preying on deer. [[Edo period]], 18th century]]

Traditionally, Japanese clothing – first the {{transliteration|ja|[[kosode]]}} and its later evolution, the [[kimono]] – did not have pockets. Though the sleeves of the kimono could be used to store small items, the men who wore kimono needed a larger and stronger container in which to store personal belongings, such as pipes, tobacco, money and seals, resulting in the development of containers known as {{transliteration|ja|}}, which were hung by cords from the robes' sashes ({{transliteration|ja|[[obi (sash)|obi]]}}).

These containers may have been pouches or small woven baskets, but the most popular were crafted boxes ({{transliteration|ja|inrō}}) held shut by {{transliteration|ja|[[ojime]]}}, sliding beads on cords. Whatever the form of the container, the fastener which secured the cord at the top of the sash was a carved, button-like toggle called a {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}. {{transliteration|ja|Netsuke}}, like {{transliteration|ja|inrō}} and {{transliteration|ja|ojime}}, evolved over time from being strictly utilitarian into objects of great artistic merit and an expression of extraordinary craftsmanship. {{transliteration|ja|Netsuke}} production was most popular during the [[Edo period]] (1603–1867).<ref name ="saip86"/>

{{transliteration|ja|Netsuke}} and {{transliteration|ja|[[inrō]]}} declined as Japanese clothes were gradually westernized from the [[Meiji (era)|Meiji period]] (1868–1912). Because of their popularity amongst Western collectors at the time, some of the greatest collections are now found outside of Japan.<ref>{{Cite web |title=International Netsuke Society - FAQ |url=https://www.netsuke.org/page-1125375 |access-date=2023-08-12 |website=www.netsuke.org}}</ref>

Today, the production of {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} continues, and some modern {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} can command high prices in the UK, Europe, the US, Japan and elsewhere. Inexpensive yet faithful reproductions are available in museums and souvenir shops.

== Etymology ==
The term {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} is formed from the characters {{nihongo3|meaning 'root'||ne}} and {{nihongo3|meaning 'attached'||tsuke}}. In [[:en:American English|American English]], the word is usually italicized, while it is usually unitalicized in [[British English]].<ref>[http://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/nets/hd_nets.htm "Netsuke: From Fashion Fobs to Coveted Collectibles"], [[Metropolitan Museum of Art]]</ref><ref>[http://www.vam.ac.uk/content/articles/n/netsuke-inro/ "Netsuke & Inro"], [[Victoria & Albert Museum]]</ref>{{original research inline|date=May 2024}}

== Forms ==
[[Image:Netsuke-p1030001.jpg|thumb|Drawing of a man wearing an {{transliteration|ja|[[inrō]]}} supported by a {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} passed through the ties of his {{transliteration|ja|[[hakama]]}}.]]

{{glossary}}
{{term|Anabori-netsuke|{{Nihongo3|or "hollowed {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}"|穴彫根付|Anabori-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|Subset of {{transliteration|ja|katabori}} which are carved out for a hollow center. Clams are most commonly the motifs for this type of {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}.}}

{{term|Kagamibuta-netsuke|{{nihongo3|or "mirror-lid {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}"|鏡蓋根付|Kagamibuta-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|Shaped like a {{transliteration|ja|manjū}}, but with a metal disc serving as a lid to a shallow bowl, usually of ivory. The metal is often highly decorated with a wide variety of metallurgical techniques.}}

{{term|Karakuri-netsuke|{{Nihongo3|or "trick/mechanism {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}"|からくり根付|Karakuri-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|Any {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} that has moving parts or hidden surprises.}}

{{term|Katabori-netsuke|{{nihongo3|or "sculpture {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}"|形彫根付|Katabori-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|This is the most common type of {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}. They are compact three-dimensional figures carved in a round shape and are usually around one to three inches high.}}

{{term|Manjū-netsuke|{{nihongo||饅頭根付|Manjū-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|A thick, flat, round {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}, with carving usually done in [[relief]], sometimes made of two ivory halves. Shaped like a {{transliteration|ja|[[manju (food)|manjū]]}} bun, a Japanese confection.}}

{{term|Men-netsuke|{{Nihongo3|or "mask {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}"|面根付|Men-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|The largest Category:Pages and Categories from Wikipedia 2]]

[[Category after {{transliteration|ja|katabori}}. These were often imitations of full-size [[Noh]] masks and share characteristics in common with both {{transliteration|ja|katabori}} and {{transliteration|ja|manjū/kagamibuta}}.}}

{{term|Obi-hasami|{{transliteration|ja|Obi-hasami}}}}
{{defn|Another elongated {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} with a curved top and bottom. It sits behind the {{transliteration|ja|obi}} with the curved ends visible above and below the {{transliteration|ja|obi}}.}}

{{term|Ryūsa-netsuke|{{nihongo||柳左根付|Ryūsa-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|Shaped like a {{transliteration|ja|}}, but carved like lace, so that light is transmitted through the item.}}

{{term|Sashi-netsuke|{{nihongo||差根付|Sashi-netsuke}}}}
{{defn|This is an elongated form of {{transliteration|ja|katabori}}, literally "stab" {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}}, similar in length to the sticks and gourds used as improvised {{transliteration|ja|netsuke}} before carved pieces were produced. They are about {{convert|6|in}} long.}}
{{glossary end}}

